#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:32
msgid "Using Xacro"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:33
msgid ""
"As its name implies, `xacro <https://index.ros.org/p/xacro>`_ is a macro "
"language for XML. The xacro program runs all of the macros and outputs the "
"result. Typical usage looks something like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:37
msgid "$ xacro model.xacro > model.urdf"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:41
msgid ""
"You can also automatically generate the urdf in a launch file. This is "
"convenient because it stays up to date and doesn't use up hard drive space. "
"However, it does take time to generate, so be aware that your launch file "
"might take longer to start up."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:45
msgid ""
"To run xacro within your launch file, you need to put the ``Command`` "
"substitution as a parameter to the ``robot_state_publisher``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:47
msgid ""
"path_to_urdf = get_package_share_path('turtlebot3_description') / 'urdf' / "
"'turtlebot3_burger.urdf'\n"
"robot_state_publisher_node = launch_ros.actions.Node(\n"
"    package='robot_state_publisher',\n"
"    executable='robot_state_publisher',\n"
"    parameters=[{\n"
"        'robot_description': ParameterValue(\n"
"            Command(['xacro ', str(path_to_urdf)]), value_type=str\n"
"        )\n"
"    }]\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:60
msgid ""
"An easier way to load the robot model is to use the `urdf_launch <https://"
"github.com/ros/urdf_launch>`_ package to automatically load the xacro/urdf."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:62
msgid ""
"from source.Capabilites.Simulation.URDF.launch.launch import "
"LaunchDescription\n"
"from launch.actions import IncludeLaunchDescription\n"
"from launch.substitutions import PathJoinSubstitution\n"
"from launch_ros.substitutions import FindPackageShare\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"def generate_launch_description():\n"
"    return LaunchDescription([\n"
"        IncludeLaunchDescription(\n"
"            PathJoinSubstitution([FindPackageShare('urdf_launch'), 'launch', "
"'display.launch.py']),\n"
"            launch_arguments={\n"
"                'urdf_package': 'turtlebot3_description',\n"
"                'urdf_package_path': PathJoinSubstitution(['urdf', "
"'turtlebot3_burger.urdf'])\n"
"            }.items()\n"
"        )\n"
"    ])\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:65
msgid ""
"At the top of the URDF file, you must specify a namespace in order for the "
"file to parse properly. For example, these are the first two lines of a "
"valid xacro file:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:68
msgid ""
"<?xml version=\"1.0\"?>\n"
"<robot xmlns:xacro=\"http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro\" name=\"firefighter\">"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:75
msgid "Let's take a quick look at our base_link in R2D2."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:77
msgid ""
"<link name=\"base_link\">\n"
"  <visual>\n"
"    <geometry>\n"
"      <cylinder length=\"0.6\" radius=\"0.2\"/>\n"
"    </geometry>\n"
"    <material name=\"blue\"/>\n"
"  </visual>\n"
"  <collision>\n"
"    <geometry>\n"
"      <cylinder length=\"0.6\" radius=\"0.2\"/>\n"
"    </geometry>\n"
"  </collision>\n"
"</link>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:93
msgid ""
"The information here is a little redundant. We specify the length and radius "
"of the cylinder twice. Worse, if we want to change that, we need to do so in "
"two different places."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:97
msgid ""
"Fortunately, xacro allows you to specify properties which act as constants. "
"Instead, of the above code, we can write this."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:100
msgid ""
"<xacro:property name=\"width\" value=\"0.2\" />\n"
"<xacro:property name=\"bodylen\" value=\"0.6\" />\n"
"<link name=\"base_link\">\n"
"    <visual>\n"
"        <geometry>\n"
"            <cylinder radius=\"${width}\" length=\"${bodylen}\"/>\n"
"        </geometry>\n"
"        <material name=\"blue\"/>\n"
"    </visual>\n"
"    <collision>\n"
"        <geometry>\n"
"            <cylinder radius=\"${width}\" length=\"${bodylen}\"/>\n"
"        </geometry>\n"
"    </collision>\n"
"</link>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:118
msgid ""
"The two values are specified in the first two lines. They can be defined "
"just about anywhere (assuming valid XML), at any level, before or after they "
"are used. Usually they go at the top."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:121
msgid ""
"Instead of specifying the actual radius in the geometry element, we use a "
"dollar sign and curly brackets to signify the value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:122
msgid "This code will generate the same code shown above."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:124
msgid ""
"The value of the contents of the ${} construct are then used to replace the "
"${}. This means you can combine it with other text in the attribute."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:127
msgid ""
"<xacro:property name=\"robotname\" value=\"marvin\" />\n"
"<link name=\"${robotname}s_leg\" />"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:132
msgid "This will generate"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:134
msgid "<link name=\"marvins_leg\" />"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:138
msgid ""
"However, the contents in the ${} don't have to only be a property, which "
"brings us to our next point..."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:141
msgid "Math"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:142
msgid ""
"You can build up arbitrarily complex expressions in the ${} construct using "
"the four basic operations (+,-,*,/), the unary minus, and parenthesis. "
"Examples:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:145
msgid ""
"<cylinder radius=\"${wheeldiam/2}\" length=\"0.1\"/>\n"
"<origin xyz=\"${reflect*(width+.02)} 0 0.25\" />"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:150
msgid ""
"You can also use more than the basic mathematical operations, like ``sin`` "
"and ``cos``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:154
msgid "Here's the biggest and most useful component to the xacro package."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:157
msgid "Simple Macro"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:158
msgid "Let's take a look at a simple useless macro."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:160
msgid ""
"<xacro:macro name=\"default_origin\">\n"
"    <origin xyz=\"0 0 0\" rpy=\"0 0 0\"/>\n"
"</xacro:macro>\n"
"<xacro:default_origin />"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:167
msgid ""
"(This is useless, since if the origin is not specified, it has the same "
"value as this.) This code will generate the following."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:170
msgid "<origin rpy=\"0 0 0\" xyz=\"0 0 0\"/>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:174
msgid ""
"The name is not technically a required element, but you need to specify it "
"to be able to use it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:175
msgid ""
"Every instance of the ``<xacro:$NAME />`` is replaced with the contents of "
"the ``xacro:macro`` tag."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:176
msgid ""
"Note that even though its not exactly the same (the two attributes have "
"switched order), the generated XML is equivalent."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:177
msgid ""
"If the xacro with a specified name is not found, it will not be expanded and "
"will NOT generate an error."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:180
msgid "Parameterized Macro"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:181
msgid ""
"You can also parameterize macros so that they don't generate the same exact "
"text every time. When combined with the math functionality, this is even "
"more powerful."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:184
msgid "First, let's take an example of a simple macro used in R2D2."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:186
msgid ""
"<xacro:macro name=\"default_inertial\" params=\"mass\">\n"
"    <inertial>\n"
"            <mass value=\"${mass}\" />\n"
"            <inertia ixx=\"${(1e-3) * mass}\" ixy=\"0.0\" ixz=\"0.0\"\n"
"                 iyy=\"${(1e-3) * mass}\" iyz=\"0.0\"\n"
"                 izz=\"${(1e-3) * mass}\" />\n"
"    </inertial>\n"
"</xacro:macro>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:197
msgid "This can be used with the code"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:199
msgid "<xacro:default_inertial mass=\"10\"/>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:203
msgid ""
"The parameters act just like properties, and you can use them in expressions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:205
msgid "You can also use entire blocks as parameters too."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:207
msgid ""
"<xacro:macro name=\"blue_shape\" params=\"name *shape\">\n"
"    <link name=\"${name}\">\n"
"        <visual>\n"
"            <geometry>\n"
"                <xacro:insert_block name=\"shape\" />\n"
"            </geometry>\n"
"            <material name=\"blue\"/>\n"
"        </visual>\n"
"        <collision>\n"
"            <geometry>\n"
"                <xacro:insert_block name=\"shape\" />\n"
"            </geometry>\n"
"        </collision>\n"
"    </link>\n"
"</xacro:macro>\n"
"\n"
"<xacro:blue_shape name=\"base_link\">\n"
"    <cylinder radius=\".42\" length=\".01\" />\n"
"</xacro:blue_shape>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:229
msgid ""
"To specify a block parameter, include an asterisk before its parameter name."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:230
msgid "A block can be inserted using the insert_block command"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:231
msgid "Insert the block as many times as you wish."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:234
msgid "Practical Usage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Build/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File/Using-Xacro-to-Clean-Up-a-URDF-File.rst:235
msgid ""
"The xacro language is rather flexible in what it allows you to do. Here are "
